dsh: reasoning:
Basic.lean looks reasonable. The `HeatSubsolutionData` structure has fields that are hypotheses about u's derivatives. `IsHeatSubsolutionOn Ω T u := Nonempty (HeatSubsolutionData Ω T u)`.

Note: no boundedness of Ω here yet. The weak maximum principle will need Ω open, bounded, nonempty, T>0, u continuous on closed cylinder, and a bound on the parabolic boundary. Let me read WeakMaximumPrinciple.lean.
dsh: reasoning:
This looks like a genuinely correct weak maximum principle. The hypotheses: Ω open, bounded, nonempty; T > 0; u continuous on the cylinder; u a subsolution; u ≤ M on parabolic boundary. Conclusion: ∀ p ∈ cylinder, u p ≤ M. This is the standard weak maximum principle.

Some subtle points:
1. `weak_maximum_principle_strict` requires `D.td x t - ∑ i, D.gxx x t i ≤ -ε` — note `hasDerivWithinAt_nonneg_of_isMaxOn_Icc` — the left derivative at maximum over [0, t₀] is ≥ 0. Good.

2. In `weak_maximum_principle`, ε := (u p - M)/(2T), and they need `u p - ε·p.2 > M`. hεbound: ε·p.2 ≤ ε·T = (u p - M)/2 < u p - M. Good. Uses p.2 ∈ [0,T].

3. `deriv_deriv_nonpos_of_isLocalMax` in SecondDerivativeTest.lean — need to check its statement. `hcoord_deriv` gives HasDerivAt of `deriv (fun r => u (...))` at q₀.1 i equal to gxx. Then `deriv_deriv_nonpos_of_isLocalMax (hcoord_max i) (hcoord_diff i) (hcoord_deriv i)`. Need to check that lemma's hypotheses match: presumably (hmax : IsLocalMax f a) (hdiff : ∀ᶠ s in 𝓝 a, DifferentiableAt ℝ f s) (hderiv : HasDerivAt (deriv f) b a) : b ≤ 0.

One subtle issue: `D.gxx q₀.1 q₀.2 i` is the second derivative of u in direction i: `gx (update x i s) t i` is the first derivative at s; so gxx is derivative of s ↦ gx(update x i s, t) i = derivative of the first partial. So gxx is ∂ᵢ∂ᵢu. And `∑ i, gxx` is the coordinate Laplacian. Good.

But wait: is `hcoord_deriv` valid? It says HasDerivAt (deriv (fun r => u (update q₀.1 i r, q₀.2))) (D.gxx q₀.1 q₀.2 i) (q₀.1 i). From `D.hasDeriv_space2 q₀.2 ht₀ q₀.1 hx₀Ω i`: HasDerivAt (fun s => gx (update q₀.1 i s) q₀.2 i) (gxx q₀.1 q₀.2 i) (q₀.1 i). And they use congr_of_eventuallyEq with the fact that for s near q₀.1 i, `gx (update q₀.1 i s) q₀.2 i = deriv (fun r => u (update (update q₀.1 i s) i r, q₀.2)) (q₀.1 i)`... wait no. Let me re-read:

```
have hcoord_deriv (i : Fin n) : HasDerivAt
    (deriv (fun r : ℝ => u (Function.update q₀.1 i r, q₀.2))) (D.gxx q₀.1 q₀.2 i) (q₀.1 i) := by
  refine (D.hasDeriv_space2 q₀.2 ht₀ q₀.1 hx₀Ω i).congr_of_eventuallyEq ?_
  filter_upwards [hcoord_mem i] with s hs
  have h := D.hasDeriv_space q₀.2 ht₀ (Function.update q₀.1 i s) hs i
  simpa [Function.update_idem, Function.update_self] using h.deriv
```

So for s near q₀.1 i, they need gx (update q₀.1 i s) q₀.2 i = deriv (fun r => u (update q₀.1 i r, q₀.2)) s. From hasDeriv_space: HasDerivAt (fun r => u (update (update q₀.1 i s) i r, q₀.2)) (gx (update q₀.1 i s) q₀.2 i) s. Note: update (update q₀.1 i s) i r = update q₀.1 i r (update_idem: updating the same index twice gives the last value). So the function equals fun r => u (update q₀.1 i r, q₀.2). Hence gx ... = deriv of that at s. That's `h.deriv`. Good — this is correct.

So the weak maximum principle looks correct. The one-sided time derivative requirement is a hypothesis, and `of_classical` shows the classical notion implies it. Fine.

